{"name":"Distant Flare","hex":"#ead1da","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"distant","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Far off (physically, logically or mentally).","Emotionally unresponsive or unwilling to express genuine feelings."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["This term needs a definition. Please help out and add a definition, then remove the text {{rfdef}}."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/distant"},{"term":"flare","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sudden bright light.","A source of brightly burning light or intense heat."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cause to burn; in particular, to burn off excess gas.","To cause inflammation; to inflame."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flare"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flare","blocks":[{"text":"A flare is a type of pyrotechnic that produces a bright light or intense heat without an explosion. Flares are used for distress signaling, illumination, or defensive countermeasures in civilian and military applications. Flares may be ground pyrotechnics, projectile pyrotechnics, or parachute-suspended to provide maximum illumination time over a large area. Projectile pyrotechnics may be dropped from aircraft, fired from rocket or artillery, or deployed by flare guns or handheld percussive tubes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/US_Army_52253_Best_Warrior_At_Night.jpg/330px-US_Army_52253_Best_Warrior_At_Night.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flare"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Flares_being_fired_from_illuminated_ships_during_International_Fleet_Review_2016.jpg/500px-Flares_being_fired_from_illuminated_ships_during_International_Fleet_Review_2016.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/